data: {"type":"reasoning","text":"The user wants to create a task application with Node.js modules ESM, HTTP native, no dependencies, HTML/CSS/JS interface, API endpoints. It also requires tests using node --test for create, complete, delete; run them and fix failures.\n\nWe need to create additional files: tasks.json probably initially empty. index.html, style.css, script.js maybe. Also tests using Node's built-in test runner. We might need package.json to specify type module and test script, maybe \"test\": \"node --test\".\n\nAlso need to ensure server.mjs is started by node server.mjs, but tests should programmatically start the server, call API via HTTP, then stop. We need to run tests as part of node --test.\n\nWe will create package.json with \"type\": \"module\" and \"scripts\": { \"test\": \"node --test\" }. But node --test doesn't use npm scripts? Actually node --test can be run as command line. But for tests to run, we need test files with extension .test.js or .test.mjs. The tests will import the server or use http module to start server.\n\nSince we already have server.mjs that starts server automatically if run directly. In tests we might import startServer and stopServer; but tests file will need to be an ES module. Node --test automatically runs *.test.{js,mjs} files. So we can create test/tasks.test.mjs.\n\nWe need to create index.html in project root; served by server.mjs static routing. tasks.json to store tasks. Should exist; server.mjs loads tasks and creates file if not exist.\n\nAlso need CSS and JS files referenced.\n\nEnsure no dependencies. Use fetch API in client to call API endpoints.\n\nImplement tests: create a task via POST, expect 201 and returned JSON; then send PATCH to complete the task; then DELETE to remove it; check responses. Also maybe check server persistence by reading tasks.json after server restart? But not necessary.\n\nTo test, tests should start server on a free port. Could import server.mjs startServer but ensure unique port. server.mjs startServer accepts port, defaults 3000. tests can start on 0 to let OS choose, but need to get port. Our startServer returns server and we can get server.address().port. Node http.Server.address returns object with port.\n\nBut since startServer listens on provided port, if we pass 0, OS picks port. We'll start it with startServer(0). Then get server.address().port. Use fetch or http.request to interact.\n\nBut tests run in Node environment; Node 26, we can use fetch global.\n\nTest steps:\n\n- import { startServer, stopServer } from '../server.mjs'. Wait need relative path? test file in same dir as server.mjs? Let's put test file in tests folder? The folder is empty; we need to create tests/test.tasks.test.mjs in same directory or subfolder. Use import relative: import { startServer, stopServer } from '../server.mjs'; if test in tests folder.\n\n- In beforeAll hook: startServer(0). afterAll: stopServer(server).\n\n- In each test: use fetch(`http://localhost:${port}/api/tasks`, {method:'POST', headers:{'Content-Type':'application/json'}, body:JSON.stringify({title:'Test task'})}). Expect status 201, parse json; get id.\n\n- PATCH: fetch(... PATCH with body {id, completed:true}). Expect 200.\n\n- DELETE: fetch(... DELETE body {id}). But for DELETE we use method: 'DELETE', body: JSON.stringify({id}).\n\nAlso we may want to verify that after deletion, GET returns empty or no task.\n\nEnsure fetch is available global; Node 18+ includes fetch.\n\nAlso need to ensure server.mjs uses body parsing by reading request body and JSON parse. Already implemented.\n\nPotential issues: In delete, we use 'DELETE', but our server.handleApi expects body parameter; we parse body always. fetch will send body content-type default application/json? Need to set header.\n\nAlso we may need to set server to export functions already defined. In server.mjs, we exported startServer, stopServer. That is fine. Ensure that the file is an ES module; it is.\n\nAlso tests need to import from correct path: tests should be in same folder? Let's create test files in the same root folder for simplicity. Use test/server.test.mjs.\n\nBetter to create tests folder inside project root: tests/tasks.test.mjs. Then import relative: import { startServer, stopServer } from '../server.mjs'; Because tests folder inside same root.\n\nThe test will need to set global fetch? Node fetch is global.\n\nAlso need to include jest-like functions? Node test uses test(), expect, etc. Node's test runner provides 'test', 'expect', 'beforeAll', 'afterAll'. We'll use them.\n\nWe also need to include top-level await? Node test supports top-level await. We'll use top-level await inside tests.\n\nPotential requirement: after startServer, we need to get port. Let's do:\n\nconst server = await startServer(0);\nconst { port } = server.address();\n\nBut server.address might return null initially until listening? But after startServer resolves, server should be listening.\n\nWe'll proceed.\n\nAlso need to ensure that server.mjs startServer returns server after listening, but uses new Promise(resolve => server.listen(port, resolve)); Good.\n\nNow we need to create tasks.json file? server.mjs will create file if not exist; tests will run create; but if tasks.json existed with previous data could influence tests. We'll remove tasks.json before running? Or tests should start with clean state. We can create empty tasks.json file before tests. But server.mjs loadTasks will read tasks.json; if file doesn't exist, it will create empty file. So tests will start with empty tasks file. But if tests run multiple times, tasks.json might persist from previous runs. For deterministic behavior, test could delete tasks.json before starting? But tests should run in isolated environment; but since we cannot modify filesystem outside tests, we could let tests use startServer(0) that reads tasks.json; after test, we can stop server and maybe delete tasks.json. But not necessary.\n\nBut to ensure tests pass, we might delete tasks.json after tests to clean; but not required.\n\nLet's create tests/tasks.test.mjs.\n\nAlso create index.html referencing script.js. Provide minimal UI but not needed for tests.\n\nLet's create CSS and JS.\n\nClient side: script.js will implement UI: fetch GET to list tasks, render list, form to add, buttons to complete, delete. Use fetch.\n\nBut no need for tests.\n\nImplement minimal HTML with container.\n\nLet's write files.\n\nProceed to create:\n\n- index.html\n- style.css\n- script.js\n- tests/tasks.test.mjs\n- package.json (with type module and test script? Not required but maybe helpful).\n\nAlso update server.mjs to export startServer and stopServer. Already done.\n\nLet's create files.\n\n"}

